eu leaders have rejected a plan to hold  talks with russia's vladimir putin  france and germany made the proposal but  it got plenty of backlash from baltic  countries at a summit in brussels  and there was another topic dividing  member states what to do about a new law  in hungary  that critics say is homophobic more than  half of eu leaders condemn the  legislation calling it discrimination  that goes against eu values well  hungary's  prime minister victor orban says he has  no intention of changing the law  rainbow-colored rage at what's seen as a  hungarian attack on fundamental rights  stole the spotlight at a european union  summit that was to be dominated by  efforts to deal with the coronavirus  crisis  and its economic consequences as he  arrived in brussels hungarian prime  minister victor orban faced  calls for his country's expulsion from  the eu  for a new law that bans any portrayal of  homosexuality to minors  many leaders confronted orban directly  in the meeting  some of them quite emotionally belgian  prime minister alexandre de crow says  he was surprised by what he called the  quasi-unanimous opposition  we have clearly pointed out to orban  this is a line in the sand  you've gone too far and this law is  unacceptable  more than half of leaders signed a  letter underscoring the eu principle of  non-discrimination over sexual  orientation and gender identity the  european commission has already begun a  legal procedure against budapest over  the legislation  another departure from the planned  agenda on russian relations  a last-minute franco-german proposal to  invite vladimir putin to a summit  that suggestion failed to gain support  to the german chancellor chagrin  we couldn't agree today to immediately  meet at the high level  at the leaders level but for me it's  important that the format of a dialogue  will be further investigated i  personally would have wished for a more  courageous step  but it's good as it is so this is  awkward lithuanian president kitanas  nauseata  is also pleased with the outcome because  the proposal failed  i think it's too early because we  so far we don't see any radical changes  of behavior of patent of behavior  vladimir putin  and to try to  engage is of course very good idea but  to engage without any deadlines without  any preconditions  of course it would be a very wrong  signal i would say  leaders did cover other subjects on  migration they focused on how to stop it  at its source  and on the coronavirus they called for  more solidarity and coordination on  efforts to fight the pandemic  and to reopen travel next up prospects  and  programs for economic recovery  let's go right to brussels our bureau  chief alexander fanon is standing by for  us there good morning alexandra  i want to ask you about the two big  topics dividing member states first of  all the question of a possible summit  with vladimir putin there was no  agreement on this issue  why is that well we have to understand  first of all that this was a last minute  pitch by  germany and france and that was  presented shortly before the summit  started  many eu leaders felt blindsided saying  why is it justified to have this idea  right now  only because the u.s president met with  vladimir putin  we have to understand that high-level  talks between eu leaders and  the russian president were suspended  after the annexation of crimea  since then the relations got even worse  so that is why  many eu leaders among them many in  eastern europe are saying it's too early  to talk about that  first we have to see a change of  behavior  and concessions from the kremlin okay  let's talk about the other topic the  lgbtq law in hungary that's very  controversial we know  viktor orban is not backing down eu  leaders many of them saying  it is homophobic and i want to play you  what the dutch prime minister had to say  for many people ask me hungary no longer  has a place in the eu  but unfortunately with the system we  have i can't or the other 26 member  states can't just tell them to leave  that needs to be done step by step and  in the meantime we hope they'll adapt  alexander that is a pretty bold  statement from the dutch prime minister  that hungary  may not have a place in the eu anymore  so how is this issue going to play out  well this is a very bold statement  indeed and i i think it's fair to assume  that other eu leaders were thinking  the same when they had this heated  emotional discussion  about a new law in hungary but we have  to say that this is of course up to  hungary to decide whether they would  like to stay in the european union or  not  and now after this very heated summit we  are sort of  to speak back to business the european  commission has announced he  that it is triggering legal proceedings  over this new legislation  in hungary however in the past we also  saw that hungary tends to ignore  court rulings but there is a new  mechanism a new rule of law mechanism  linked to eu funds it's not in place  at the moment but it will be soon so we  definitely  expect to see a decision by the european  commission  to freeze eu funds uh this being  distributed to hungary  if there is a lack of democratic values  in this country  you called it a heated summit i mean  what did these disagreements tell us  about the state of unity  in the european union well i think  it's telling us a lot about the problems  and challenges the european union is  facing  it was meant to be a club of democracies  you know a counter model to  dictatorships and and  authoritarian regimes elsewhere not  within the bloc and now the european  union is  facing authoritarian tendencies within  the club  and it's just uh the whole thing is  telling us that the european union is  not well equipped  to to to counter such authoritarian and  democratic  moves measures within the european union  okay last question alexander considering  those struggles do you think there's any  prospect of a concrete outcome  on these topics well we actually have a  few concrete outcomes uh we have  uh um the decision to look look into  further punitive measures with regard to  russia the european union wants  to work closely together with the  countries  who can help to stem migration and  the eu leaders are quite united when it  comes to covet and how to  fight future pandemics our brussels  bureau chief alexander fundament  reporting for us there thank you very  much  you
 

